Proposed consensus patch for terrain-check's connectivity rows on fortified
Field and Grand boards (not applied: it changes boards, and the core and the
corpus belong to another step this wave). Apply from the repo root with
`git apply packages/tails-and-scales/sim/notes/keep-connectivity.diff`.

What fails is not the keep. On seeds 1-500 the 320 failing boards (160 Field,
160 Grand) are exactly the boards whose centre draw lands in [0.5, 0.8),
where `fortified` stands the ruined tower ring round the centre objective:
its doors are about 1.25" wide, no squad (r 0.89-1.18) walks in, and no
r 1.18 squad reaches a cell within 4.18" of the objective. On all 502 keep
boards an r 1.18 squad walks up either stair and holds the objective from
the keep's top, within HIGH of its floor. F8b found the same of the tower on
`open` and dropped it there for the rock box; F7a's merge then put Field and
Grand on `fortified`, whose centre rule still has it.

The patch gives `fortified` a `largeCentre`, read in place of `centre` on a
table larger than classic's: the keep under 0.5 as before, the rock box under
0.8 where the tower stood. The one centre draw is the same, so the keep and
open ground stand on the same boards and only the tower boards change; the
Skirmish's fortified boards (and so the fort/ corpus) are untouched.
Measured with it applied: terrain-check exits 0, connectivity 2,000 of
2,000; parity 70 of 70 byte-identical (no corpus battle is a fortified Field
or Grand one); data-check passes. RULES_ID moves (core/version.js hashes
SETS), so a peer on older code opens such a match as 'skew'; whether to
bump CORE_VERSION beside it is the landing step's call. Still to run when it
lands: P-prop (P-height and P-level on Grand-sized boards), scale-check's
--fortified, army-sim's Field and Grand gates on fortified, and a
data-check refusal for a bad `largeCentre`; DESIGN's fortified set block
gains the field.
(The simpler alternative, `centre: [['keep', 0.5], ['rockbox', 0.8]]` for
every size, also moves the Skirmish's tower boards, about 30% of fort/'s
seeds, and so re-records fort/.)

